{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "However, till date and to the best of our knowledge, no specific microbial taxa have been consistently and uniquely associated with Alzheimer's. Furthermore, case-control studies with smaller samples may be affected by low power and confounding factors which obscure true biological signals. With several case-control studies being published in recent years, it seems an appropriate next step to pool the studies together in a meta-analysis to derive robust insights with potential for clinical impact.",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the current state (inconsistent taxa findings, small sample/low power, confounding) and uncertainty, and states rationale to pool studies for more robust insights."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "With several case-control studies being published in recent years, it seems an appropriate next step to pool the studies together in a meta-analysis to derive robust insights with potential for clinical impact. In this work, we systematically reviewed case-control studies of AD and MCI patient gut microbiomes. The outcomes of gut microbiome studies namely, changes in α -diversity, β - diversity, changes in relative abundance, and linear discriminant analysis effect sizes, were studied to identify the microbial taxa consistently impacted by gut dysbiosis. Growing evidence relating gut dysbiosis to Alzheimer's and other forms of dementia emphasizes the timely nature of this article.",
      "reason": "The authors explain the importance and need for the review (to pool recent studies via meta-analysis to derive robust, clinically relevant insights) and specify the outcomes addressed."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No evidence was found in the provided text that discusses existing similar systematic reviews or explains the need relative to prior reviews; the introduction does not cite or compare to previous reviews."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_9",
      "quote": "Wesearched MEDLINE, Cochrane, EBSCO, EMBASE, and Scopus, for case-control metagenomic and 16S studies of Alzheimer's disease (AD) and mild cognitive impairment (MCI) in humans.",
      "reason": "This review focuses on observational case-control microbiome studies rather than interventions; therefore describing an intervention mechanism is not applicable."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "In this work, we systematically reviewed case-control studies of AD and MCI patient gut microbiomes.",
      "reason": "The review does not assess complex interventions or delivery contexts, so a logic model for interventions is not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "80"
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "This study has two outcomes: primary and secondary. The primary outcomes explored the changes in α -diversity and relative abundance of microbial taxa, which were analyzed using a variance-weighted randomeffects model. The secondary outcomes focused on qualitatively summarized β -diversity ordination and linear discriminant analysis effect sizes.",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly states the review objectives by naming the primary and secondary outcomes (changes in α-diversity and relative abundance; qualitative summaries of β-diversity and LEfSe results), which defines the questions the review addresses."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_9",
      "quote": "Wesearched MEDLINE, Cochrane, EBSCO, EMBASE, and Scopus, for case-control metagenomic and 16S studies of Alzheimer's disease (AD) and mild cognitive impairment (MCI) in humans.",
      "reason": "The review concerns observational case-control microbiome studies and outcomes (diversity and taxon abundance), not intervention effects; therefore PICO-style planned comparisons for interventions are not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_10",
      "quote": "Studies were eligible if they assessed the gut microbiomes of human patients with AD or MCI with metagenomic sequencing and reported outcomes such as α -diversity, β -diversity ordination, relative abundances of various taxa, and linear discriminant analysis effect sizes (LEfSe). Eligible study designs were case-control and intervention studies (with baseline sampling). Patients had to meet well-defined diagnostic criteria such as DSM (Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders) or NIA-AA (National Institute on Aging and Alzheimer's Association) guidelines. If patients self-reported decreases in cognition and memory (corroborated by a caregiver) without meeting the diagnostic criteria for AD, they were considered to have MCI. Controls were cognitively normal (CN) subjects, reasonably matched to the AD and MCI cohorts in age, gender, years of education, and lifestyle. Exclusion criteria included antibiotic use within two weeks of sample collection and the presence of confounding conditions such as depression, cancer, or any other genetic/neurological disorders.",
      "reason": "Study characteristics used for eligibility are explicitly specified: populations (AD, MCI, CN), outcomes (alpha/beta diversity, taxa, LEfSe), study designs (case-control, interventions baseline), diagnostic criteria (DSM, NIA-AA), matching criteria for controls, and specific exclusion criteria (antibiotics, comorbid conditions)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Wesearched MEDLINE, Scopus, EMBASE, EBSCO, and Cochrane for AD gut microbiome studies published between Jan 1, 2010 and Mar 31, 2022.",
      "reason": "The date range for eligible reports is specified (2010–2022), but the evidence does not state eligibility restrictions by language or explicit publication/report status (e.g., gray literature, unpublished studies) so report-characteristic criteria are only partially described."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_24",
      "quote": "S3 Table. Study selection. List of full-text reports screened with reasons for exclusion. (PDF)",
      "reason": "The manuscript indicates that reasons for exclusion of full-text reports were recorded (S3 Table) and the PRISMA flow figure shows explicit exclusion reasons (e.g., 'Metagenomics not performed on AD group', 'Results not published yet'), but the main text does not clearly and explicitly distinguish exclusions due to outcomes not being measured versus outcomes not being reported in a dedicated statement; thus the distinction is only partially documented in the provided evidence."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_16",
      "quote": "Twelve studies analyzed AD cohorts and ten studies analyzed MCI cohorts. ... The four studies reported primary outcomes at baseline and were therefore included in the study. The remaining were case-control studies.",
      "reason": "Groups used in syntheses are specified (AD cohorts, MCI cohorts, and cognitively normal controls) and outcomes tied to these comparisons are described (primary outcomes for pooling, and which studies contributed), linking groupings to the review objectives."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": "text_10",
      "quote": "Exclusion criteria included antibiotic use within two weeks of sample collection and the presence of confounding conditions such as depression, cancer, or any other genetic/neurological disorders.",
      "reason": "Notable eligibility restrictions are stated, but the manuscript does not provide a rationale or justification for these restrictions in the retrieved evidence (no explanation of why e.g., two-week antibiotic exclusion was chosen), so rationales are not provided."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

